From b522767b2d1d425647c781fa19c04ac7a025f59d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 17:43:20 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 211/212] DOS-PORT: patch 0211 -- legacy pathfinder: reuse the
 walkable-mask analysis while the mask is unchanged

is_route_possible() copies the walkable mask and makes two full passes over
it (the second column-major) to measure each area's average path width and
normalise every walkable pixel to 1, before the flood fill from the source.
Those passes depend only on the mask's pixels and MaxGranularity, yet ran on
every call: twice per walk request whose target is not itself walkable (the
nearest-walkable retry calls it again on the same mask), and again for every
later request while nothing on the mask had moved. On DOSBox-X at fixed
40000 cycles they were 44 ms per call, 88 ms of each 98-231 ms request
replaying the 13 walk requests 5 Days a Stranger made on the 486 (dosags
RT2a), where those requests stall the game 250-370 ms.

The analysis is now kept with a copy of the mask it was made from; a call on
a byte-identical mask (same size, same MaxGranularity) restores
walk_area_granularity[] and the normalised mask from it and goes straight to
the flood fill, which is split out unchanged. Same inputs, same outputs: the
routes are unchanged (witness: route hashes, 339 requests). The cache costs
two copies of the mask (2 x 64 KB at 320x200) and a compare per call, done a
32-bit word at a time because DJGPP's memcmp is a byte loop (~13 ms per
64 KB compare at fixed 40000 cycles). It is freed with the route finder.
